\section{Introduction}\label{sec:local-peephole-intro}

Local and peephole optimizations are the simplest and most widely applied class of compiler optimizations. They examine a small window of code---a single instruction, a basic block, or a short sequence of instructions---and apply local transformations that improve the code without requiring global program analysis. Despite their limited scope, these optimizations can yield significant performance improvements, often accounting for 10--30\% of the total speedup achieved by a compiler.

The term ``peephole optimization'' was coined by Tesler and Enea~\cite{tesler1968improving} in 1968 to describe a technique that examines the generated code through a small ``peephole'' and replaces instruction sequences with more efficient equivalents. Modern compilers apply thousands of peephole rules, many of them machine-specific.

\section{Constant Folding and Propagation}\label{sec:constant-folding}

\subsection{Constant Folding}\label{sec:constant-folding-detail}

\emph{Constant folding} evaluates expressions involving only constants at compile time, replacing the expression with its computed value.

\begin{definition}[Constant Folding]\label{def:constant-folding}
\textbf{Constant folding} replaces an expression $e_1 \mathbin{\text{op}} e_2$ with the value $v_1 \mathbin{\text{op}} v_2$ when both $e_1$ and $e_2$ are compile-time constants with values $v_1$ and $v_2$.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Constant Folding]\label{ex:const-fold}
\begin{verbatim}
  x = 3 + 4;         →  x = 7;
  y = 2 * 3.14;      →  y = 6.28;
  z = "hello" + " world";  →  z = "hello world";
  a = 10 > 5;        →  a = true;
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\subsection{Constant Propagation}\label{sec:constant-prop-detail}

\emph{Constant propagation} replaces uses of a variable with its known constant value.

\begin{definition}[Constant Propagation]\label{def:constant-prop}
\textbf{Constant propagation} replaces a use of variable $x$ with the constant $c$ if $x$ is known to have value $c$ at that program point.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Constant Propagation]\label{ex:const-prop}
\begin{verbatim}
  x = 5;
  y = x + 3;    →  y = 5 + 3;  →  y = 8;
  z = x * 2;    →  z = 5 * 2;  →  z = 10;
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\subsection{Implementation}\label{sec:const-impl}

Constant folding and propagation are typically implemented as a single pass:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Maintain a mapping from variables to their known constant values.
  \item For each assignment $x = e$:
    \begin{itemize}
      \item If $e$ is a constant expression (all operands are constants), fold it and record $x \mapsto v$.
      \item Otherwise, record $x \mapsto \top$ (unknown).
    \end{itemize}
  \item For each use of $x$: if $x \mapsto c$, replace with $c$.
  \item At control-flow merge points: if $x$ has different values on different paths, set $x \mapsto \top$.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Algebraic Simplification}\label{sec:algebraic-simplification}

\emph{Algebraic simplification} applies algebraic identities to reduce expressions.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\hline
\textbf{Pattern} & \textbf{Replacement} \\
\hline
$x + 0$ & $x$ \\
$x - 0$ & $x$ \\
$x \times 1$ & $x$ \\
$x \times 0$ & $0$ \\
$x / 1$ & $x$ \\
$x \mathbin{\&} 0$ & $0$ \\
$x \mathbin{|} 0$ & $x$ \\
$x \wedge x$ & $0$ \\
$x \mathbin{|} x$ & $x$ \\
$x \mathbin{\&} x$ & $x$ \\
$\neg(\neg x)$ & $x$ \\
$x - x$ & $0$ \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Common algebraic simplification rules.}
\label{tab:algebraic-rules}
\end{table}

\subsection{Strength Reduction}\label{sec:strength-reduction}

\emph{Strength reduction} replaces expensive operations with cheaper equivalents.

\begin{definition}[Strength Reduction]\label{def:strength-reduction}
\textbf{Strength reduction} replaces an operation with a computationally cheaper operation that produces the same result.
\end{definition}

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\hline
\textbf{Expensive} & \textbf{Cheaper} \\
\hline
$x^2$ & $x \times x$ \\
$x / 2$ & $x \gg 1$ (for unsigned $x$) \\
$x \times 2^k$ & $x \ll k$ \\
$x \bmod 2^k$ & $x \mathbin{\&} (2^k - 1)$ \\
$x \times c$ (constant $c$) & Shift-and-add sequence \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Common strength reduction transformations.}
\label{tab:strength-reduction}
\end{table}

\begin{example}[Strength Reduction for Multiplication]\label{ex:sr-mul}
Multiplication by a constant can be replaced by shifts and adds:
\begin{verbatim}
  x * 10  →  (x << 3) + (x << 1)    // 8x + 2x = 10x
  x * 7   →  (x << 3) - x            // 8x - x = 7x
  x * 15  →  (x << 4) - x            // 16x - x = 15x
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\section{Peephole Optimization Patterns}\label{sec:peephole-patterns}

Peephole optimization examines a sliding window of instructions and applies local replacement rules.

\subsection{Redundant Load/Store Elimination}\label{sec:redundant-load-store}

