\section{Redundant Instruction Elimination}\label{sec:redundant-elimination}

\subsection{Dead Code Elimination (Local)}\label{sec:local-dce}

Local dead code elimination removes instructions whose results are never used within the same basic block.

\begin{definition}[Local Dead Code]\label{def:local-dead}
An instruction $s$ is \textbf{locally dead} if its result is not used by any subsequent instruction in the same basic block.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Local Dead Code]\label{ex:local-dead}
\begin{verbatim}
  x = a + b;    // dead if x is not used later in this block
  y = c * d;    // y is used below
  z = y + 1;
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\subsection{Move Elimination}\label{sec:move-elimination}

\emph{Move elimination} (or \emph{copy elimination}) removes redundant copy/move instructions.

\begin{definition}[Move Elimination]\label{def:move-elim}
A move instruction $\texttt{mov R1, R2}$ can be eliminated if:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $\texttt{R1}$ and $\texttt{R2}$ already contain the same value, or
  \item $\texttt{R1}$ is not used before being redefined.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

