\subsection{Implementation Architecture}\label{sec:peephole-arch}

Modern compilers organize peephole optimizations as a collection of \emph{pattern-action} rules:

\begin{definition}[Peephole Rule]\label{def:peephole-rule}
A \textbf{peephole rule} consists of:
\begin{itemize}
  \item A \textbf{pattern}: a sequence of instructions (possibly with wildcards).
  \item A \textbf{guard}: a condition that must hold for the rule to apply.
  \item A \textbf{replacement}: the instruction sequence to substitute.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Peephole Optimization Engine}
\label{alg:peephole}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Instruction sequence $I$, rule set $R$
\Ensure Optimized instruction sequence
\State $changed \gets \text{true}$
\While{$changed$}
  \State $changed \gets \text{false}$
  \For{each window $W$ of instructions in $I$}
    \For{each rule $r \in R$}
      \If{$W$ matches pattern of $r$ and guard of $r$ holds}
        \State Replace $W$ with replacement of $r$
        \State $changed \gets \text{true}$
      \EndIf
    \EndFor
  \EndFor
\EndWhile
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{historical}
Peephole optimization was introduced by Tesler and Enea in 1968~\cite{tesler1968improving} as a technique for improving the quality of generated code. The idea was to examine the output of the code generator through a small ``peephole'' and replace inefficient instruction sequences with better ones. Modern compilers like GCC and LLVM apply thousands of peephole rules, many of them generated automatically from machine descriptions. The concept has evolved to include not just instruction-level patterns but also basic-block-level and even function-level transformations.
\end{historical}

\section{Combining Local Optimizations}\label{sec:combining-local}

In practice, local and peephole optimizations are applied iteratively until no more transformations are possible. The order of application can affect the final result:

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Iterative Local Optimization}
\label{alg:iterative-local}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Function $F$
\Ensure Optimized function
\State $\mathit{changed} \gets \text{true}$
\While{$\mathit{changed}$}
  \State $\mathit{changed} \gets \text{false}$
  \ForAll{basic blocks $B$ in $F$}
    \State Apply constant folding in $B$
    \State Apply algebraic simplification in $B$
    \State Apply strength reduction in $B$
    \State Apply peephole rules in $B$
    \State Apply dead code elimination in $B$
    \If{any transformation was applied}
      \State $\mathit{changed} \gets \text{true}$
    \EndIf
  \EndFor
\EndWhile
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Interaction Between Optimizations}\label{sec:local-interaction}

Local optimizations interact in important ways:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Constant folding can expose dead code (if a branch condition becomes constant).
  \item Strength reduction can expose common subexpressions.
  \item Dead code elimination can expose new constant propagation opportunities.
  \item Algebraic simplification can enable further constant folding.
\end{itemize}

\section{Machine Description for Peephole Optimization}\label{sec:machine-desc}

Modern compilers use machine descriptions to specify peephole rules declaratively:

\begin{definition}[Peephole Pattern Specification]\label{def:peephole-spec}
A \textbf{peephole pattern specification} defines rules in a declarative format:
\begin{verbatim}
  (define_peephole
    (set (reg:SI R1) (plus:SI (reg:SI R2) (const_int 0)))
    (set (reg:SI R1) (reg:SI R2))
    "add with 0 is a move")
\end{verbatim}
\end{definition}

