\section{Checking Condition Codes Before Deleting an Identity}

An instruction that leaves its destination register unchanged may still produce flags. For example, removing \texttt{add eax, 0} on x86 can change the next conditional branch. A peephole matcher needs a check on implicit operands as well as on the visible result.

\textbf{Inputs and assumptions.} Consider one basic block and a matched identity candidate. The target provides conservative sets of flag bits read and definitely overwritten by each instruction, plus the IR's live flag bits on block exit. A conditional write is not a definite overwrite. Before examining flags, prove the candidate preserves all nonflag state and effects, including any effects on register units that are absent from its printed operands. Debugging or instrumentation requirements are part of this contract.

\begin{sudocode}[Delete only dead flag definitions]
\Require Candidate $i$, block suffix $S$, exit flag set $L$
\Ensure Safe deletion decision
\ForAll{$j\in S$ in reverse order}
  \Let{L}{L\setminus\Call{DefiniteWrites}{j}}
  \Let{L}{L\cup\Call{Reads}{j}}
  \State \Comment{Read-before-write uses become live}
\EndFor
\If{nonflag deletion checks fail for $i$}
  \State \Return keep
\EndIf
\If{$L\cap\Call{MayChange}{i}\ne\emptyset$}
  \State \Return keep \Comment{An observable implicit result}
\EndIf
\State \Return delete
\end{sudocode}

\textbf{Example.} On x86-64 let \texttt{eax} equal 0, the high half of \texttt{rax} also be 0, and the incoming zero flag be clear. Executing \texttt{add eax, 0; jz L} sets the zero flag and takes the branch. The scan encounters \texttt{jz} and marks the zero flag live, so the addition is kept. With an intervening \texttt{cmp ecx, 7}, the comparison defines that flag before the branch reads it. If no other relevant bits are live and the high half is proven zero, the addition can be deleted.

\textbf{Invariant.} At each suffix boundary, $L$ includes any flag whose incoming value can be observed before a definite overwrite. An unknown instruction must be treated conservatively. One backward pass can compute the boundaries for all candidates in the block; scanning anew for each candidate can give quadratic behavior.

\section{Summary}\label{sec:local-peephole-summary}

Local and peephole optimizations are the workhorses of compiler optimization. They are simple to implement, fast to execute, and effective at eliminating common inefficiencies in generated code. Constant folding and propagation eliminate compile-time-computable expressions. Algebraic simplification and strength reduction replace expensive operations with cheaper equivalents. Peephole patterns eliminate redundant instructions, optimize branches, and exploit machine-specific features. Local optimizations interact and should be applied iteratively. Modern compilers use machine descriptions to specify peephole rules declaratively. Despite their limited scope, these optimizations are essential for producing efficient code.

